    "rich_text_standard": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "f_sub": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "The fish",
            "subheading_size": "subtitle-md",
            "secondary_color": true
          }
        },
        "f_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why a fatty fillet is the right one to cure",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "f_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A miso cure pulls moisture out. A lean fillet gives up too much and goes firm and dry; a fatty one has enough in reserve to come out silky after two days, which is why black cod became the classic and why King Salmon works just as well.</p><p>Fat also carries the caramelisation. The sugars in the glaze need something to sit against, and that deep lacquered edge is only possible on a fillet rich enough to take it.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
