    "faq_miso": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_onepan": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I really have to wait 48 hours?",
            "text": "<p>For this version, yes &mdash; the time is what firms the flesh and drives the flavour past the surface. At 24 hours you still get a very good piece of fish, and under about 4 hours it is a glaze rather than a cure. Do not go past 72 hours; the salt starts to win and the texture turns dense.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_doneness": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What temperature should I pull it at?",
            "text": "<p>Around 130&deg;F. It climbs to roughly 150&deg;F while it rests on the sheet, which lands it tender rather than firm. Take it out while the very centre still looks slightly translucent.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_burn": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My glaze burns before the fish is cooked. What now?",
            "text": "<p>Almost always too much marinade left on. Scrape the fillets properly and brush on one thin fresh layer instead. If it still colours too fast, drop the oven rack a level rather than cutting the time short &mdash; you want the glaze and the fillet to finish together.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long do the boxes keep?",
            "text": "<p>Three to four days sealed in the refrigerator. The salmon is designed to be eaten cold or at room temperature, so there is no reheating step; just pull a box out and go.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_tomatoes": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What kind of miso should I use?",
            "text": "<p>White or awase miso is what this is built around &mdash; mellow and slightly sweet. Red miso works if that is what you have, but it is considerably saltier, so use about two-thirds as much and taste the glaze before the fish goes in.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia King Salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in cold water for 30&ndash;60 minutes. Not at room temperature and not in warm water. There is a <a href=\\\"/blogs/seafood-recipes/best-ways-to-defrost-sushi-grade-seafood-for-sashimi-and-cooking\\\">full guide to defrosting</a> if you want the detail.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_onepan",
